issues.problematic_code_snippet:
abstract class TauriActivity : WryActivity() {
  var pluginManager: PluginManager = PluginManager(this)
  override val handleBackNavigation: Boolean = true

findings.body:
The new `setup` closure binds `let handle_ref = &handle;` and then calls `AppPlugin(*handle_ref)`. That moves the `PluginHandle` out of a shared borrow, which requires `Copy`. `PluginHandle` derives only `Debug` and implements `Clone` manually (`crates/tauri/src/plugin.rs`), so the code fails with E0507 on every Android build of `tauri`. The sibling `PathPlugin` setup in `crates/tauri/src/path/plugin.rs` shows the intended pattern: pass the owned handle straight into `manage`.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/src/app/plugin.rs" lines="135-144"}

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri/src/app/plugin.rs" lines="139-140"}
        _app.manage(AppPlugin(handle));

The underscore-prefixed `_app` and `_api` names are also used here, which is misleading. Rename them to `app` and `api`.

`onBackButtonPress` is a public `@tauri-apps/api` export, and the build.rs change adds `register_listener` and `remove_listener` to the default `core:app` permission set. But the `app` plugin's Rust `invoke_handler` on desktop and iOS does not implement these commands. Calling `onBackButtonPress` on those platforms will reject with a command-not-found error, even though the app's default permissions say the call is allowed. The JS docstring only says "on Android", and the changelog says "Added app > onBackButtonPress for Android back button handling", which is easy to miss.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/build.rs" lines="164-170"}

Two options: make `onBackButtonPress` a no-op that resolves to an inert `PluginListener` off Android, or keep the rejection but document it clearly in the TSDoc. Either way, the default-permission exposure of commands that don't exist on desktop should be reconsidered, for example by scoping the two ACL entries to Android only.

`TauriActivity` now sets `override val handleBackNavigation: Boolean = true`. This assumes WryActivity exposes that property and that its own back callback is compatible with the new plugin callback. I could not verify WryActivity's source in this checkout. If WryActivity registers its own `OnBackPressedCallback` (which I believe it does, gated by this flag), there are now two callbacks. `OnBackPressedDispatcher` invokes callbacks in LIFO order, so whichever is registered last wins. If WryActivity's callback wins, the JS `back-button` listener will never fire, and `AppPlugin`'s callback is effectively dead. If the flag already defaults to `true`, the line is a no-op and should be removed.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/mobile/android-codegen/TauriActivity.kt" lines="12-16"}

Before merging, confirm the WryActivity contract for `handleBackNavigation` and test on a device that a registered listener receives events and that the default goBack still works when no listener is registered.

`AppPlugin.exit` is annotated `@Command`, but there is no Rust command, no JS export, and no `build.rs` entry for it, so no permission is generated for it. The changelog says the PR adds "exit and back button press event", but nothing exposes `exit` to the frontend. This is dead code in practice, and a reviewer will reasonably expect it to work.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/mobile/android/src/main/java/app/tauri/AppPlugin.kt" lines="49-53"}

Either wire it up (a Rust command, a `build.rs` entry, and a JS `exit()` export), or remove it until it is needed. The mismatch between the changelog and the code should also be fixed.

The constructor accepts a generic `Activity`, but `init` casts unconditionally with `(activity as AppCompatActivity)`. Any host that uses a plain `Activity` will get a `ClassCastException` when the plugin is instantiated. The `Plugin` base class takes `Activity`, so this is a surprising contract. Other plugins in this module only use `activity` through the base class.

::diff{file="crates/tauri/mobile/android/src/main/java/app/tauri/AppPlugin.kt" lines="44-47"}

If AppCompat is a hard requirement, make the constructor parameter `AppCompatActivity` (or use an `as?` cast and skip registration with a log line when it is not an AppCompatActivity).

The PR bumps `wry` from 0.53.2 to 0.53.4 in `crates/tauri-runtime-wry/Cargo.toml` and in `Cargo.lock`, and it also shifts several `windows-sys`/`windows-targets` edges in the lockfile. None of this is needed for the back-button feature, and the lockfile churn makes the review harder. A wry minor-patch bump can change webview behavior on every platform, which is a separate risk from the Android feature.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-runtime-wry/Cargo.toml" lines="19-22"}

Move the dependency bump to its own PR, or explain in the description why it is required. Keep the feature PR to the back-button changes.

Once any JS listener is registered, `AppPlugin` stops going back in the WebView and stops finishing the activity. The app is then solely responsible for handling back presses, including exiting. This is a reasonable design, and it matches the Capacitor reference the author cited. But the JS docs do not say so, and there is no documented way to exit the app from JS (see the unwired `exit` command). A developer who registers a listener and forgets to handle the back press will find the back button appears dead.

::code{file="packages/api/src/app.ts" lines="263-275"}

Document the contract in the TSDoc: when a listener is registered, the default back behavior is suppressed, and the handler is responsible for navigation and exit.
